    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!--
      The VexelRay stack's versions, in one place: every library an application can depend on, at the version this
      framework was built and tested against. An application imports it and names its dependencies without versions:

        <dependencyManagement>
            <dependencies>
                <dependency>
                    <groupId>dev.vexelray.framework</groupId>
                    <artifactId>vexelray-bom</artifactId>
                    <version>...</version>
                    <type>pom</type>
                    <scope>import</scope>
                </dependency>
            </dependencies>
        </dependencyManagement>

      Versions only (ruled 2026-11-16). No parent, so that what an import brings is exactly this list and nothing
      inherited with it, and no parent role either: an application keeps its own parent. It manages the transitive
      edges too, so an application that names one library and reaches twenty gets the twenty this list says rather
      than whichever a dependency happened to name.

      What it does not carry, because no BOM can: plugin versions (vexelray-build-maven-plugin and
      vexelray-gui-icons-maven-plugin are named with a version by the application), and native-image flags and
      metadata, which travel in each library's own jar under META-INF/native-image/<groupId>/<artifactId>/, so an
      application that depends on a backend inherits exactly what that backend needs.

      Left out on purpose: demos, benches, the build-time Maven plugins, the test-only architecture guard,
      ottermate (a tool run by path, never a dependency), and supirvast's vastir-preview, which brings LWJGL.

      Each sibling's version is one property, the version on its develop branch. A sibling that moves its version
      moves it here too; VexelDesktopTest checks the framework's own entries against this repository's version.
    -->
